# ArgonOS - the CYD: what every build for it shares, and the CYD as a monitor.
#
#   argon target esp32-cyd       this file: no radios at all, every byte to
#                                the heap - a screen at the end of a wire
#   argon target esp32-cyd-net   + sdkconfig.esp32.cydnet: Wi-Fi with the
#                                smallest buffers, the shop's terminal
#   argon target esp32-cyd-md    + cydnet + sdkconfig.esp32.cydmd: 9 KB more
#                                IRAM for the Mega Drive
#
# The two lines met on 1 Nov 2026: main had the CYD as a monitor, the shop's
# line (cyd-sdfix) as a terminal with the radio, and each had the same file.
# What makes the whole system better whatever the board is for - the heap, the
# card's sector size, the flash on four lines, the timer task's stack - is
# here; what is for one job is in that job's overlay.
#
# The monitor's case: the CYD's job is to be a screen at the end of a wire,
# and the thing that job needs is memory: a 320x240 frame is 150 KB, and a
# frame that cannot be assembled whole appears on the glass in pieces.  With
# the radios in the image the heap tops out around 125 KB free - and 92 in one
# piece - which is not enough however the code is arranged.
#
# `wifi off` does not help.  Switching the radio off hands back about 27 KB of
# what it had allocated; the rest is the stacks, the buffers and the static
# data the Wi-Fi and Bluetooth stacks reserve at link time, and only leaving
# them out of the image gets that back.
#
# What is lost with them: uploading files over HTTP, which is how a driver gets
# onto this board in one second instead of a minute of hex over the console.
# That is a real cost and it is worth paying here, because the console path
# still works and the picture does not.
CONFIG_ARGON_ENABLE_NET=n
CONFIG_ARGON_NET_WIFI=n
CONFIG_ARGON_ENABLE_BLE=n
CONFIG_BT_ENABLED=n

# ESP-IDF's own timer task: 3584 by default and measured at 529 bytes used, so
# 2048 keeps a 4x margin.  This one is a build option and cannot be a line of
# SYSTEM.CFG - IDF creates the task before any file is read.
CONFIG_ESP_TIMER_TASK_STACK_SIZE=2048

# The card's file system at the card's own sector size.  4096 is for a FAT on
# internal flash (wear levelling), which this board does not have - C: is
# littlefs.  At 4096 every one of the eight files the mount reserves carries a
# 4 KB cache, and mounting the card took 44 KB of the 136 the board has free,
# from the middle of its one 84 KB block (measured 28 Oct 2026, with the Mega
# Drive needing 64 KB of it in one piece).  At 512, about 5 KB.
CONFIG_FATFS_SECTOR_512=y
CONFIG_FATFS_SECTOR_4096=n
# ...and the wear-levelling layer's too: FatFs sizes every buffer to the larger
# of the two (FF_MAX_SS = MAX(SD, WL) in ffconf.h), so 512 for the card alone
# changed nothing - measured, 44 KB both ways.  Nothing here uses wear levelling.
CONFIG_WL_SECTOR_SIZE_512=y
CONFIG_WL_SECTOR_SIZE_4096=n

# The two-ended heap in place of ESP-IDF's TLSF: small blocks from the top of
# the fullest arena, large ones from the bottom (twoend_core.h).  Measured on
# this board 29 Oct 2026 over one session (boot, radio off, card out and in, an
# application): largest pieces after `wifi off` 80/11/10 KB with TLSF, 93/8/7
# with this - the kernel's boot placed with the long-lived, the radio started
# last, the SPI staging buffer moved into what the radio left (memshape.h).
# The Master System emulator, refused under TLSF for want of a 13 KB arena,
# starts; the Mega Drive still gets its 128 KB.
CONFIG_ARGON_HEAP_TWOEND=y

# The flash on four lines (QIO), not two (DIO).  Every cache miss on the
# ESP32 is a 32-byte line read over SPI, ~450 CPU cycles in DIO at 80 MHz;
# QIO halves the data phase.  The CYD's flash (Zbit, id 5e 4016) takes the
# bootloader's default QE setting.  Measured 31 Oct 2026 with MD32 on the
# panel: ECCO 35 -> 55 whole pictures a second, its 68000 12.2 -> 8.9 ms a
# frame; Thunder Force IV 19 -> 29 pictures, its sound 618 -> 521 ms a second;
# a row to the glass 31 -> 18 us.
CONFIG_ESPTOOLPY_FLASHMODE_QIO=y

# The sound driver without its I2S codec path: this board's sound is the
# chip's own converters, and the I2S driver's DMA handlers sit in IRAM whether
# or not a channel is opened - 331 bytes that left the Mega Drive two pages
# short when that path arrived from main (1 Nov 2026).
CONFIG_ARGON_AUDIO_NO_I2S=y
